This file discusses how the proposed Z-machine standard is likely to
impact Infocom and goes on to discuss some other interpreters for the
platforms which Infocom supports.

The Standard
============

Graham Nelson has recently proposed a standard for the Z-machine.  A
major motive for this is the severe problem which game authors face
when presented with a range of interpreters of wildly varying
accuracy.  With the help of quite a few others, Graham has tried to
nail down exactly what Infocom intended the opcodes to do, based where
possible the evidence provided by Infocom story files and genuine
interpreters.

Of course, the available evidence (especially for V6) is a little thin
on the ground, which is why the errors in some interpreters are
understandable.  Screen handling, especially in the presence of colour
and proportional fonts, seems particularly tricky to get right.

As far as standard conformance goes, Infocom 1.7 was hopeless; it
seems to have been about as far from conformance as possible while
still having a vague chance of being fixed.  For example the header
handling in the original ITF sources is almost pure Version3.  The
subtleties required for indicating availability of fonts, colour and
sound were not present at all.

Infocom 1.8 should be better; the revamp needed to add colour support
has helped the header handling somewhat.  However, real-time support
is still missing and seems difficult to implement in a portable
manner.  Equally, the `multiple output-streams' mandated by the
standard have only the vaguest resemblance to the four-layer ITF
output model.

In summary, this interpreter is *not* conformant and needs quite a lot
of work before it can be.  (It looks like 2.0 might stand a chance,
which at the current release rate of every six months means Infocom
might be conformant some time (very) late in 1997.)


Acorn
=====

When !Infocom was first ported to the Acorn, the other main Infocom
interpreters on the Archimedes were Frank Lancaster's Zip program and
Edouard Poor's desktop interpreters (called !AUInfocom and !Zip.)

Zip is blindingly fast, and very compact, but runs outside the desktop
using the standard system font and plays only version 3 games.  The
font size this implies is enough to make it almost unusable on an A4.
Frank Lancaster has also released ZipDebug, which is a combined
debugger and interpreter; the main point of interest is prototype
support for version 6 games.

Edouard Poor's interpreters (at least early versions) run in the
desktop, but fail to display a cursor and fail to handle backspacing
properly.

Since !Infocom 1.7 was released, Kevin Bracey has released his port of
Mark Howell's Zip interpreter.  This has quite a few advantages over
Infocom:

* Faster screen redraw (presumably due to a better 'fit' between his screen
  model and the Acorn font screen code.)
* Full version 5 support including the real-time support.
* Version 6 support for playing the late `graphical' games.
* Conformant with the proposed Z-machine specification, due in large
  part to using the cleaner Zip interpreter core.
* Colour and sound support.

This raises the obvious question of why !Infocom is still being
supported on the Acorn.  There are four reasons:

* Save file compatibility.  From time to time I play Infocom games on
  all the machine Infocom runs on, and it is very useful to be able to
  transfer saved positions freely.
* I have some users who also have multiple platforms and like the same
  portability.  (And will complain loudly if I take it away.)
* !Infocom uses slightly less memory, at least on older machines.  (If
  the ToolBox is in ROM, the situation may be reversed.)
* It is mostly a no-brain operation to keep the Acorn version in step.

Conclusion: If you need the portability !Infocom provides, or you have
a really old machine, you might prefer !Infocom to !Zip2000.  You ought
to give !Zip2000 a good look anyway though.


Psion
=====

The only other Infocom interpreter I have seen on the Series3 is
InfoPlay ported by Oliver Wagner, and based on Mark Howell's ZIP
interpreter rather than the ITF interpreter.  Currently, InfoPlay
suffers 3 main disadvantages; it uses more memory (typically 6 to
10K), it uses simple fixed-width fonts for output, and the OS
integration is slightly buggy; try ESCaping from a save dialogue, or
killing the task from the System screen.

(InfoPlay used more memory than the 1.4x version of Infocom; the new
memory handling in 1.7 means that for larger games like Trinity the
gain is even more noticeable.  A naive port of Zip or ITF would be
unable to fit the resident pages of Trinity into the 64K small memory
model of the Psion.)

(Brief advert: There is also a commercial version of Infocom for
Psion, sold under the name InfoZip.  This is supplied in both 3A and
S3 versions, both of which are smaller than shareware version since
they are essentially preregistered, along with a printed manual and a
companion application InfoStat. It is available bundled with either or
both of the Lost Treasures packages from Lacegem on (0)61-792-2871.)


Unix
====

The main infocom interpreters in use under UNIX appear to be simple
ports of the ITF and ZIP interpreters, usually running under the
curses screen handling library.  The other X11 based interpreters seem
to have fairly crude screen handling, but tracking down all the
available interpreters is not practical.


Windoze
=======

I haven't played with any of the Windoze based Zip ports.  I have used
the DOS port of Zip occasionally, but not in anger.  My beta-testers
report that they prefer Infocom to the other Windoze interpreters they
have tried.

The main pluses for Infocom are

* Dynamic window resizing and font selection.
* Save file compatibility with the other ports, including Psion.
* Command line editing.
* Double clicking a saved position can start the correct game
  and restore automatically.

